<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanny of the Maroons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Queen Nanny, Granny Nanny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nanny of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windward-maroons">Windward Maroons</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in the early 18th century, during the First Maroon War against the British. Remembered as a spiritual leader and military strategist, she led the community at Nanny Town in the Blue Mountains. In 1975 she was declared a National Hero of Jamaica, the only woman to hold the title.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nanny-of-the-maroons.jpg" alt="Nanny of the Maroons" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jamaica1962 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanny_of_the_Maroons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
